1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:43,120
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard.

7
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:51,200
My guest today is Alper Tanner, Head of Performance Marketing at a Stealth Mode App Studio, working on productivity and utility apps.

8
00:00:51,920 --> 00:01:00,800
Alper has been in mobile growth for over a decade, leading UA and Martech efforts across a wide range of verticals and managing eight figure annual budgets.

9
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:12,320
On the podcast, I talk with Alper about the competitive advantage of ignoring some best practices, the risk of drawing false conclusions when researching competitor ads.

10
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:17,920
And why poor metrics are just facts until proven problematic.

11
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,040
Hey Alpert, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:22,240
Yeah, thank you for having me.

13
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:23,520
It's been a long time coming.

14
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:40,960
You and I first met in person at MAU years ago and we've kind of talked off and on about getting you on the podcast, but recently we were talking about a lot of your contrarian ideas, just that you think a little bit differently and like to challenge the kind of rules of thumb.

15
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,480
And I thought that would be a special fun thing to talk about.

16
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:46,640
And so let's let's start it off with that.

17
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,440
The podcast shares a ton of like, rules of thumb and like, hey, try this, don't try that.

18
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,800
Like, oh, this didn't work for me, so you probably shouldn't do it.

19
00:01:55,360 --> 00:02:00,000
And on the revenue cap blog, we share all sorts of articles like that.

20
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:07,200
But you went through as you and I have been talking, preparing for this episode, and you kind of ripped holes in some of the like rules of thumb.

21
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,680
So at a high level, why don't you just start off?

22
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,400
Like, how do you think about these kind of best practices and rules of thumb?

23
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:24,720
There are definitely best practices and rules of thumb that you should stick to, I don't know, 80% of the time, but that doesn't mean they're universal.

24
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,120
But the data isn't universal for every single case.

25
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:44,880
So if I were the app developer listening to all these ideas of do's and don'ts, I would prioritize it based on my current situation, data, and I would still try them just to see that it also doesn't work.

26
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:54,560
Of course, it has to be reasonable, but the idea is that your data should show you that instead of, oh, we heard someone said X, Y, Z type of approach.

27
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:05,600
So I think this applies across the way you test the creatives, the way you test the incrementality, the way you test your bidding strategy.

28
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:14,000
So I think these are all meaningful tests to a certain extent, depending on what stage you are in.

29
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:22,400
If you don't have your own learnings, of course, it's smarter to leverage others' learnings and it can just help you to prioritize.

30
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:26,560
If you hear something didn't work for twenty people, just deprioritize it.

31
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:36,400
But it doesn't mean, Oh, forget about this because it's not going to work for you because algorithms are constantly changing and improving and whatever.

32
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:40,320
someone tried six months ago can work today.

33
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:46,240
And then we see that it's happening across multiple platforms within the platforms as well.

34
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:52,320
Like the campaign A that or like specific targeting that was working really well, stops working.

35
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:55,200
This is like a day to day thing, all your creative dies, right?

36
00:03:55,440 --> 00:04:00,560
Like where the creative that worked six months ago that you had to relaunch, it works great again.

37
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:05,680
So also it potentially looks much better now because of the late conversions and stuff.

38
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:07,520
So it's not black and white.

39
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:25,200
I think when you hear such advice, you should definitely like take them as suggestions, but when it comes to execution and prioritization, you should do that based on your own data because you don't know the history of that particular account and why particular thing didn't work.

40
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:36,000
And there are several or many reasons why a particular setup would work for one market or platform versus not.

41
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:39,680
And that's why they're all individual cases.

42
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:46,560
There are definitely, as I said, best practices that are universal, but I would always challenge them.

43
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:48,640
I take them as, okay, great.

44
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:50,000
Let me try it.

45
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:51,600
Yeah.

46
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:55,440
Well, I think you've just created a new rule of thumb.

47
00:04:55,920 --> 00:05:17,840
I like that, that you should spend eighty percent of your time on the best practices and learn because, like you said, if twenty people tried it and it failed, maybe it's going to fail for you, but then take twenty percent of your energy, your budget, your efforts and do the exact opposite of the best practices and figure out for yourself why they learn.

48
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:24,720
And to your point, like, you know, if you're early and you don't have a lot of resources, you don't want to over index on being a contrarian.

49
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:27,360
But I mean, that's the whole thing, right?

50
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:31,920
It's that there's alpha in the best practices and that's why they're the best practices.

51
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:37,200
But then there's also alpha in the things people aren't trying or the things that certain people failed in.

52
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:45,440
But you're going to succeed in that for something that's very specific to your app or your niche or your market or something else like that.

53
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:47,680
So yeah, the new rule of thumb.

54
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:53,840
Follow the best practices eighty percent of the time and try crazy stuff twenty percent of the time.

55
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:54,400
I like that.

56
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:58,640
Let's go through some of these more specific contrarian takes.

57
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:08,720
And one of the ones we were discussing is that sometimes you just need to take something as a fact and not consider it a problem until it becomes a problem.

58
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:17,440
And the example you gave me, and I'd love for you to elaborate on this is that if your trial conversion is low and you're like, Oh, trial conversion being low is a problem.

59
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:18,480
Well, is it a problem?

60
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:20,240
Like, are you getting cheaper installs?

61
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:20,960
Tell me about that.

62
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:28,240
Yeah, like typically people would say like, Oh, we have, I don't know, installed to start trial ratio, I don't know, five percent.

63
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:33,360
And, you know, usual healthier benchmark is more like 15 to 20 percent and such.

64
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,560
And I'm like, Okay, what, what have you done?

65
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:38,160
Like, what have you tried?

66
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:40,800
And how did you interpret the results?

67
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:42,480
How did you build on those results?

68
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:44,800
They're like, oh, we didn't because it's a problem.

69
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:48,640
So that's not a problem, it's just a fact.

70
00:06:48,800 --> 00:07:06,160
I think something becomes a problem after you systematically tested, iterate on the results and try many different ways from, let's say, radical to iterative, and you're still stuck at the same level, then I think I can consider that as a potential problem.

71
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:12,240
Instead, you should always see what you can do or understand the root cause.

72
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:24,000
So diagnose any factual problem that you consider as a problem and then come up with an action plan and then measure the results to see the impact.

73
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:25,520
It's as easy as that.

74
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:40,400
It might sound very like straightforward, but I can tell you like many people bypass this kind of step by step funnel in figuring out stuff because when you follow such an approach, you realize that, oh, actually, it's not a problem.

75
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:46,000
Like, or I don't know, it's as simple as people say, like, oh, we tried, I don't know, snap and it didn't work.

76
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:47,600
I'm like, okay, how did you try it?

77
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:51,120
Oh, we spent, I don't know, five k in a week and it didn't work.

78
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:54,480
I'm like, yeah, that's not a problem that snap didn't work.

79
00:07:54,720 --> 00:07:59,840
You just didn't test the channel in a proper way that is optimal for the channel.

80
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,640
You shouldn't test the channel just for a week.

81
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:07,680
And the same goes for, I don't know, creatives to channels or the markets.

82
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:10,080
Oh, it's too expensive for this market.

83
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,240
Or that channel didn't work.

84
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:15,840
So people like to come to conclusions fast.

85
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:23,920
And I think because of the priorities, like because of this scarcity kind of mindset.

86
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:28,400
Like they want to reach conclusions really fast and they want to move on to the next.

87
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:37,360
So when they're in this kind of mindset, I think then they're missing on testing things properly.

88
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:56,480
Like I think a lot of people focus on the execution, which is on paper great, but I believe the success of a test is at least fifty percent dependent on how you plan it, how you strategize it, how you hypothesize it, and how you execute it.

89
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:58,560
And execution is just the what part.

90
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:05,600
Maybe it's even sometimes less than fiftyy percent, but basically how you execute matters more than you execute or not.

91
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:09,120
Sometimes people also say, Oh, we tried, I don't know, let's say look alike.

92
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:16,160
It didn't work because the way they tried look alike was at ten percent, run it for a week, that's it.

93
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:21,200
No seed testing, no different ranges of testing and such.

94
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,320
You can't just call the whole thing, it doesn't work.

95
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:44,240
If you don't slice and dice and try different things, that's kind of my approach and that's how I deal with day to day business to understand and prioritize it based on, okay, how can I do say I tested something and what information I need to call the shots that it doesn't work or it does work?

96
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:53,920
So this kind of thinking, I think, will force you to think of all the preliminary steps on what you should be considering and so on.

97
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:56,880
I like that kind of root cause thinking.

98
00:09:57,440 --> 00:10:05,920
I actually did a talk at MAU a couple of years ago about the top three subscription app metrics that really matter.

99
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:12,720
And that was kind of the thrust of my talk was that you need to look at the whole funnel, not just the app.

100
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:14,160
Not just individual metrics.

101
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:36,320
And like what you're saying, it's like if you have a low trial conversion rate or if you have a low install to trial rate or if you focus overly on any one metric and you're like, oh, this is a problem and you like grind and grind and grind on that quote unquote problem, when maybe you can get like really cheap installs and the really cheap installs are why you have a low conversion.

102
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:37,600
But hey, that's not a problem.

103
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:42,000
It's not a problem when you get really cheap installs and your conversion rate is not great.

104
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:48,880
Like that's maybe just a natural fact of your business that for whatever reason you're getting incredibly cheap installs.

105
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:59,520
And the example I always use with this is like, you know, my apps have been featured a ton by Apple over the years and getting featured is fantastic because it's totally free, zero cost of install.

106
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:00,240
But guess what?

107
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:03,280
Those cohorts convert really poorly.

108
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:05,760
So do I have a conversion problem?

109
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:06,480
I don't know.

110
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:16,560
I know that those cohorts don't convert very well, but like, should I overly focus on that or should I be more focused on top of funnel and like finding other ways?

111
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:27,120
And so a great reminder to not get overly focused on any one specific thing to the exclusion of all the different moving parts in the funnel.

112
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:30,240
And I like the way you phrased it too, like, it's just a fact.

113
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:31,120
That's a fact.

114
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:32,240
Right now, that's a fact.

115
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:33,040
Is it a problem?

116
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:33,680
We don't know.

117
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:34,320
It's a fact.

118
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:35,680
Find out if it's a problem.

119
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:37,120
Don't just assume it's a problem.

120
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:37,600
Yeah.

121
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:44,160
So another thing you mentioned you've been thinking a lot about lately is creative interpretation.

122
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:51,600
And we had Eric Sufford on the podcast recently and he's intentionally a bit provocative saying like, don't even try.

123
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:53,520
Don't even try to interpret the creatives.

124
00:11:53,840 --> 00:12:00,880
But you were telling me like, you do think there are things that you could learn from trying to interpret why things won, why they didn't.

125
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:08,240
So what is your process for looking at the winning creatives and like finding some value in that process?

126
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:20,160
I think looking at the output of a creative performance definitely matters because that's how you will iterate and find the next winners and or the losers.

127
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:25,120
It is important to look at the relative metrics as well.

128
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:33,360
So maybe what he's referring to is more like don't take it as your source of truth because platform is doing the modeling and such.

129
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:50,240
But at the end of the day, the output is slash the performance of the creative matters and the approach should be more like what can we learn from it, regardless of the output in a way, like good or bad, it's all about learning.

130
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:57,120
Okay, this creative for whatever reason performed better and other creative performed for whatever reason worse.

131
00:12:57,760 --> 00:12:58,480
Let's understand why.

132
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:00,560
What is the hook rate?

133
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:01,440
How is the thumbnail?

134
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:02,320
What is the audio?

135
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:03,760
What is the background and this and that?

136
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:12,560
So once we understand and then we start changing one variable at a time, then it becomes a lot clearer on how we can.

137
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:14,560
engineer the success, right?

138
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:21,360
I mean, that's the whole point of that's how you create winners from the winners because you have a winning concept.

139
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:40,320
And thanks to the interpretation of the output, you're able to create more winners because you look at the relative metrics that's a guidance for what you should be testing and what you should not be testing anymore because whatever you do in that particular case doesn't work.

140
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:49,280
However, I have seen from the same concept, like the third variation worked, but the first and second faileded.

141
00:13:49,440 --> 00:13:50,160
It's the same concept.

142
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:52,160
It's just slightly different messaging and such.

143
00:13:52,880 --> 00:14:05,200
So that's why you would always test with few variations just to avoid such cases where where you don't just jump to conclusions of a whole concept and so on.

144
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:06,240
I think that's also important.

145
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:09,360
So there could be, of course, false positives and so on.

146
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:10,960
Like, you know, nothing is perfect.

147
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:17,440
But also, I'm not also saying like, oh, you should trust algorithm blindly and stuff like that.

148
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:24,960
What I'm just saying is like, we should be able to interpret the results and then drive conclusions to shape the results.

149
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:27,360
The next creative iterations based on that.

150
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:29,920
So that's when you need to interpret the output.

151
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:42,960
And then what matters at the end of the day is your success rate and not the amount of creatives because I think LinkedIn nowadays is full of, oh, you know what, we just tested 500 creatives.

152
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:45,200
Oh, no, we tested 700.

153
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:46,320
How much did you test?

154
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:55,440
It's kind of a race that is not so meaningful because those numbers alone don't matter if your success rate is very low.

155
00:14:55,840 --> 00:15:00,000
I would more look at your cost per successful creative.

156
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:04,480
That is beating the BAU ads performance.

157
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:09,360
And it doesn't matter if you test twenty versus two hundred.

158
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:18,320
Of course, okay, twenty versus two hundred might matter, but it doesn't matter if you're testing a very high amount of creatives with a little success.

159
00:15:18,640 --> 00:15:35,680
It matters more if you test less amount of creatives, stronger hypotheses, stronger investment from analyzing and hypothesizing way with a higher success rate that what matters more than just the total number of creatives that you're testing.

160
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:46,080
I see potential issues when the creative amount is on the higher end because then the mindset is like, hey, look, I just have to test these.

161
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:47,840
Like, let's just move on.

162
00:15:49,200 --> 00:15:54,080
And then having less emphasis on why it worked, why it didn't work, and so on.

163
00:15:54,480 --> 00:16:00,960
And then sometimes there's a disconnect because typically a company that is testing 500 creatives a month, like, tend to have a team.

164
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:11,840
And usually there are, I don't want to call it silos, but the guy who is running the UA is not doing the old briefs of the creatives and then analyzing and then giving the briefs and all that typically.

165
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:15,680
In the smaller startups, yes, but in the bigger teams, there's that.

166
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:30,960
And then, so then the UA person's job is just to run them and maybe analyze them if they don't have the analyst team, then to give the creative, let's say the optimization manager to put them into briefs and get the next production.

167
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:56,240
So when the quantity is so high, like I think the level of detail and sometimes also the quality of analyzing and shaping the next input is also being jeopardized a bit because of the high quantity, which is not necessarily a win or something to be proud of alone if your success rate is low because also then the mindset shifts like, hey, look, I have 500 creatives.

168
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:58,640
I can't spend 5K on all of them.

169
00:16:59,120 --> 00:17:04,480
Sometimes this gets into, oh, we only spend $100 on these creatives and that's it.

170
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:06,800
Okay, but how did you decide on that threshold?

171
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:12,240
And I think finding that threshold is such an important thing for every account.

172
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:15,680
For example, there's no universal number that I would give.

173
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:22,720
It's very different from business to business depending on your CPI, depending on your other CPX metrics.

174
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:39,200
But what I would suggest is that to look at your cumulative spend and cumulative CPX metrics, whether you're testing on a CPI or Cosper trial or just Cosper subscription and such, and see when they stabilize.

175
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:50,080
So because I see a lot of accounts with high spend, they're sometimes just spending 10,000, 20,000, only to realize, oh, this is actually not a good creative.

176
00:17:50,240 --> 00:17:51,360
I'm like, oh really?

177
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:53,520
I could tell it after 500 bucks.

178
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:54,480
Like, look at the data.

179
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:55,360
It was already bad.

180
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:56,720
Like, it was already screaming.

181
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:58,000
Like, why did you spend so much?

182
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:24,080
So in order to avoid that inefficiency and scaling it more efficiently, you could easily look at cumulative spend and cumulative performance over time and to see where your data stabilizes, because we're not going after the statistical significance, like no one has kind of money for that, but we also don't want to spend just twenty, thirty bucks to see or we don't want to just let the algorithm decide which one to pick.

183
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:36,960
So there, the problem is the following, like just because algorithm decides to deliver one creative over others, doesn't make the algorithm the same.

184
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:39,840
The other creatives, bad creative.

185
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:45,680
I think that's such a misconception or interpreting in a, I would say, wrong way.

186
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:52,000
Like that particular creative that got the 90% of the spend was maybe lucky.

187
00:18:52,480 --> 00:19:02,480
We call it like lucky five thousand impressions because meta does some sort of a decision making around first five to ten thousand impressions.

188
00:19:03,280 --> 00:19:12,640
And if one of those five thousand people who saw the ad didn't engage or whatever signals that meta didn't receive, then it will not deliver anymore, but it will deliver.

189
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:15,920
to the other one, which just got maybe one, two clicks and stuff like that.

190
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:40,160
So, and then I've seen that multiple times where you take out that dominant ad from that ad set and all the other ads like suddenly bloom and you're like, Oh, like they were also great ads, but because of the other better ad or however you want to call it, and which is not necessarily better ad as well, because sometimes meta call the shots way too early.

191
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:41,680
No conversions, nothing.

192
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:43,200
Like it doesn't even give a chance.

193
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:48,560
So in those cases, we don't call that creator that got all the spend successful and all the rest.

194
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:50,800
bad labeling.

195
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:57,120
I think this is not universal and this is really, I think, case by case basis.

196
00:19:57,360 --> 00:20:00,640
I've also seen like ad sets with like fifty ads live.

197
00:20:01,040 --> 00:20:02,640
Oh, let's see what algorithm likes.

198
00:20:02,800 --> 00:20:05,920
And we will run it for a week and then we will see what happens.

199
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:07,920
And then what happens in that week?

200
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:10,000
Ninety percent goes to the same ad.

201
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:18,480
And because they have these rules of time, oh, we run it for seven days and at a certain, I don't know, daily budget.

202
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:21,680
But then that's useless when ninety percent of the budget goes to one creative.

203
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:22,880
Like that's not a test.

204
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:27,760
Like you should in those cases, for example., break the rules and like, hey, look, this reached my threshold.

205
00:20:28,080 --> 00:20:29,520
This is my decision threshold.

206
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:38,400
Now I want to see what other ads are going to perform for that minimum threshold that you decide based on your cumulative spend and cumulative performance over time.

207
00:20:38,960 --> 00:20:39,040
Yeah.

208
00:20:39,360 --> 00:20:50,800
So that's why like, there's no one rule that is valid for all, but I personally try to get spend on the other creatives.

209
00:20:50,960 --> 00:20:54,640
If I see a dominant one, I believe that every creative should have an equal chance.

210
00:20:55,040 --> 00:20:57,600
So I democratize a bit within the ad set, right?

211
00:20:57,920 --> 00:21:04,240
And because I've seen it countless times that the decision that made by Meta was way too early.

212
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:08,160
Yes, we trust the algorithms, but we want to guide the algorithms.

213
00:21:08,640 --> 00:21:20,080
When we see a 80, 90 percent of a dominant asset, let it be a creative, let it be a country and such, you're not fulfilling the potential of the other variables.

214
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:21,360
And I think this is important.

215
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:33,840
For example, if you put the US with all the other smaller, let's say, European countries, there's a 90 percent chance that at least 70 percent of the spend will go to the US because of its size and everything.

216
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:41,360
Also, you should never always tier by like CAC or LTV, depending on what you're optimizing towards.

217
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:44,800
And ideally without those dominating variables.

218
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:50,160
And if there's a dominating one, then you can always take it out, test it separately, give a chance to the rest.

219
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:58,800
Because it's not meaningful to have, I don't know, let's say like just a couple of thousand dollars spent with a really good CAC, really good ROAS.

220
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:12,240
Well, if you can't scale that, and then you try to scale by increasing that existing campaign's budget, but that country that you wanted to actually scale is not increasing its spend necessarily.

221
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:18,160
Because if it's getting dominated by the other bigger countries or based on whatever logic that's depending on your setup.

222
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:24,560
So in those cases, then yes, you can experiment with the value base, the rules and stuff like that.

223
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:28,960
And that we can talk about later in the testing and the learning side of things.

224
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:37,120
What's the tactical move then there when you do have that quote unquote winning creative that's getting ninety percent of the spend.

225
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:48,000
Is it better to pull the other creatives out because that ad set already knows how to target that specific ad to the right people to the right geography and everything else?

226
00:22:48,560 --> 00:22:53,440
Is it better to pull the quote unperforming ads and give them a chance somewhere else?

227
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:57,600
Or is it better to pull the performing ad into a different set?

228
00:22:58,320 --> 00:23:01,040
What's the best way to actually implement that?

229
00:23:01,760 --> 00:23:09,680
It depends on whether this creative is in a creative test ad set versus in a BAU ad set.

230
00:23:09,920 --> 00:23:20,320
If it's in a BAU ad set, then I would create another BAU ad set and then I would include those creatives that were under delivering into the new one.

231
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:42,960
And I wouldn't touch that something that is working well, but if it is within the creative testing adset and if it already reached my threshold, then I would already pause it and then I would put it on the bench to get ready to upload to BAU adsets whenever the team will do the creative refresh.

232
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:54,160
So it is case by case basis, but in general the rule of thumb is don't touch if something is working well, but depends on what case we're talking about.

233
00:23:54,320 --> 00:23:54,400
So.

234
00:23:54,720 --> 00:23:55,840
Yeah, yeah, that makes sense.

235
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:00,320
I did want to step back and not to put words in Eric's mouth.

236
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:09,120
Maybe his point is more to not overly focus on a single hypothesis for why a creative won.

237
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:13,200
So like going back to the whole, like, you know, should you learn from creatives?

238
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:18,800
Because I do want to dig a little deeper into kind of how you think about what you can learn.

239
00:24:19,760 --> 00:24:25,600
And the example he used was you look at a creative that has a dog in it and you're like, oh, like people love dogs.

240
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:27,760
And so that's why the creative won.

241
00:24:28,160 --> 00:24:45,760
And you maybe get overly focused and then to your point, then you go create two hundred new assets all with dogs, but maybe it wasn just that it was something cute and a cat one would perform just as well or a panda one would perform even better or an ocelot, like who even knows, right?

242
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:49,920
How do you think about forming a hypothesis around that?

243
00:24:50,160 --> 00:25:09,920
Because it seems like that's really the important step is, is not being overly confident in why you think something won, but instead just allowing it to help you form a hypothesis for other things to test based on a multitude of reasons why that creative could have won.

244
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:10,320
Yeah.

245
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:12,800
I mean, you should never just produce two hundred variables.

246
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:15,120
200 variations of just from one signal.

247
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:16,880
I think it's always in phases.

248
00:25:17,200 --> 00:25:18,960
So let's take the dog example.

249
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:21,200
Okay, let's have the same dog.

250
00:25:21,840 --> 00:25:22,720
Let's have a puppy.

251
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:23,920
Let's have another breed.

252
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:25,200
Let's have another animal.

253
00:25:25,520 --> 00:25:28,160
So just to test and see if it's the animal or not.

254
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:31,920
So what we want to understand is what makes the difference?

255
00:25:32,480 --> 00:25:32,960
Is it the dog?

256
00:25:33,280 --> 00:25:34,000
Is it an animal?

257
00:25:34,320 --> 00:25:35,120
Is it something else?

258
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:41,440
And once we figure that out, then we would slowly roll out the production and so on.

259
00:25:41,680 --> 00:25:43,760
So it's never about, oh, the dog won.

260
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:45,920
Now we need 200 variations of the dog.

261
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:47,840
It's more about, okay, like, is it the dog?

262
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:48,720
We want to validate that.

263
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:54,720
So in every step we want to do that and we want to do in both directions.

264
00:25:55,280 --> 00:25:58,320
That's why we have both iterative concepts and the radical concepts.

265
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:00,640
Iterative would be, oh, let's put another breed.

266
00:26:00,800 --> 00:26:01,680
Let's put two dogs.

267
00:26:01,760 --> 00:26:03,200
Maybe it's even more effective.

268
00:26:03,920 --> 00:26:14,000
And, but you know, in the radical one, you can have completely different hypotheses than what doesn't work based on what works today, but also based on other sources.

269
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:21,680
So like when it comes to what creative to test, I think you should utilize both the first party data and the third party data.

270
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:23,600
It's not always about the first party data.

271
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:44,880
So first party data meaning like your meta creative output, that's one variable that then that's a valid, let's say, input, but you also have, let's say, for particular copy, like, or let's say if you're testing particular, let's say, visuals and such that also exist in your app, right?

272
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:51,360
Okay, what is the most popular thing in the app that is more valuable for the users?

273
00:26:51,760 --> 00:26:58,640
You basically look at within your dataset, like you look at your within your in app analytics, what you can learn, what resonates with users.

274
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:01,680
You look at your high LTV users like why do they stay around?

275
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:02,960
What do they use the most?

276
00:27:03,200 --> 00:27:04,640
What messaging resonates with them?

277
00:27:05,200 --> 00:27:10,160
And then because our goal in performance marketing is to find more of those high LTV users.

278
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:20,960
So testing like test results from test campaigns is one variable, but in up analytics data is another data input that we can utilize.

279
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:28,960
And then we have the third party data where we have competitor concepts, what works for competitors.

280
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:35,600
We have overall trending memes or concepts or audios and such.

281
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:40,480
And then you could have category level concepts.

282
00:27:40,640 --> 00:27:47,600
They're not really direct competitor concepts, but other big players that are doing well within your category.

283
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:48,400
What are they doing?

284
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:50,240
How do they advertise their product?

285
00:27:50,720 --> 00:27:52,000
So what can you learn from them?

286
00:27:52,320 --> 00:27:57,520
So then you bring and merge all these datasets together, then you decide on, okay, you know what?

287
00:27:57,680 --> 00:28:04,640
Of course, you give a lot more weight to the metatest results, but it's not a 100%, hey, this is it, guys, dogs, let's go.

288
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:06,160
So it's about merging that.

289
00:28:06,320 --> 00:28:16,640
So when you hypothesize the next iterations, it also should come from what worked and what didn't work, not only for you but also for others.

290
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:22,800
You can check the MetaEd library to see how long competitors are running a particular ad and how many variations of that.

291
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:32,800
If there is one concept with fifteen variations and that is a particular, the only one that has fifteen variations compared to others having only two and three, you should be able to interpret that.

292
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:36,240
Probably that creative is performing well for them.

293
00:28:36,400 --> 00:28:38,000
That's why they're exploring more angles.

294
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:55,440
So it is important to analyze and put enough resources and attention to the planning part of the experiment, exactly because of this way and not just act like, I don't know, growth bro and hey, this worked well, let's do two hundred of them and so on.

295
00:28:55,600 --> 00:29:00,640
So I think this balanced effort planning and executing matters here.

296
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:04,640
How do you think about analyzing competitor ads though?

297
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:15,920
Because to your earlier point, there's a lot of people out there just following the playbook and creating fifteen variations because they formed a bad hypothesis around.

298
00:29:16,480 --> 00:29:24,640
So how do you actually dig into these competitor ads and try and figure out, like, if it is actually working for them?

299
00:29:25,120 --> 00:29:37,440
And that's something I just see throughout the industry broadly, is that it's too easy to over index on something you observe without the data and say, Oh, this must be working.

300
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:40,800
These fifteen creatives must be working because they've created fifteen variations.

301
00:29:41,360 --> 00:29:45,920
That may have been like a dumb move and you just don't realize how poorly it's performing.

302
00:29:46,240 --> 00:29:50,000
And they don't even realize how poorly it's performing because they don't have it instrumentated well.

303
00:29:50,480 --> 00:29:52,080
They're not tracking deeper in the funnel.

304
00:29:52,240 --> 00:30:09,600
So, like, how do you think about looking at competitor data and getting any insights from that when you don't have the full picture of why, how, what their process is, how good their stuff is instrumentated, how smart their people are, et cetera, et cetera.

305
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:10,080
Yeah.

306
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:24,800
So anything you see online, let's say, should be only as inspiration and not treated as this because I've also seen and heard people say, we tried it, it didn't work, but I don't know, leather is using it.

307
00:30:25,360 --> 00:30:29,440
Like, so then they justify and then they defend, oh, but it worked for them.

308
00:30:29,840 --> 00:30:35,520
Like, so as you said, like, it doesn't mean anything for the brand exactly because of the reasons that you mentioned.

309
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:39,120
So it should be just treated as a inspiration, as another input.

310
00:30:39,360 --> 00:30:46,720
That's why I talk about a pool of inputs that will shape together the next iterations.

311
00:30:47,200 --> 00:30:50,800
It's never about, oh, let's copy one to one, whatever compare is doing.

312
00:30:51,040 --> 00:30:52,800
Oh, let's copy the strand.

313
00:30:53,280 --> 00:31:04,080
It comes down to how I can customize this to my own personas and in my own ways based on what worked for me before and what didn't work for me before.

314
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:14,880
So at the end, this needs to go all in that processing machine that considers the personas, the past performance, the current performance and all your learnings.

315
00:31:15,280 --> 00:31:16,480
And then you test it.

316
00:31:16,560 --> 00:31:18,640
So it's never about, oh, compare is doing this.

317
00:31:18,800 --> 00:31:18,960
Great.

318
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:19,760
Let's do the same.

319
00:31:20,080 --> 00:31:24,640
I think that's probably why many people are getting upset.

320
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:30,320
I don't know when they just copied the same paywall or the same creative and then find out, oh, it doesn't work for us.

321
00:31:30,640 --> 00:31:32,400
Well, why should it work?

322
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:37,520
It's different audience, different funnel, different pricing, different messaging, different promises.

323
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:47,920
So although, for example, on the web funnels, like many of them look very alike, especially in the health and fitness space for many years, and then everyone copies from each other.

324
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:53,040
So like pretty much a lot of the health and fitness web funnels are similar now.

325
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:59,600
Some are missing, of course, some, I don't know, smaller elements here and there, but the conceptually like they're the same.

326
00:32:00,080 --> 00:32:02,960
At the end of the day, you should just run to see.

327
00:32:03,200 --> 00:32:12,800
So they should be treated as inspiration and as a variant in your whole test hypotheses and not as a test hypotheses itself.

328
00:32:13,200 --> 00:32:13,280
Yeah.

329
00:32:14,080 --> 00:32:28,480
And then one other kind of elephant in the room in all this is making sure that you're actually instrumenting things well and actually testing well and can actually interpret the results properly.

330
00:32:28,880 --> 00:32:32,800
Vahe recently had a tweet that I thought was amusingly provocative.

331
00:32:33,040 --> 00:32:42,080
He said, I won't quote it exactly, but it was something like, like, half the people who say the blinkest paywall works for them, just are not testing it properly.

332
00:32:42,960 --> 00:32:59,280
So how do you think about, like, in all this experimentation and forming hypothesis and like running all these tests, how do you think about, like, even just validating your own process and testing and tooling and, and that side of things to make sure you're even drawing the right conclusions from the data?

333
00:32:59,520 --> 00:32:59,600
Yeah.

334
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:09,840
Yeah, I mean, that's also, I think, what we covered earlier, like, fifty percent of the issue with something working or not is about how they tested.

335
00:33:10,480 --> 00:33:23,840
So I can, like, totally get like why he mentions that because I hear like similar stories, like, oh, we tested X, Y, Z pricing because competitors are the same, but our conversion rate dropped and our revenue dropped.

336
00:33:24,080 --> 00:33:30,240
Like, you shouldn't take this kind of stuff as the hard truth or your strategy.

337
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:34,800
It should be only as a variant and as a source of inspiration.

338
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:44,880
And in terms of how to interpret the results like everyone has their own in-app analytics tool or their third-party tool, depending on what tests we're talking about.

339
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:50,080
If you're talking about, let's say, paywall tests and stuff like in-app analytics is the way to go.

340
00:33:50,640 --> 00:34:00,320
If you're talking about creative tests and so on, then we're talking about MMP as a source of truth, relatively speaking here.

341
00:34:00,960 --> 00:34:08,720
But if you're talking about incremental impact of a channel, like we're not looking at the last click, obviously.

342
00:34:09,360 --> 00:34:12,320
So that's also one thing that.

343
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:18,240
that there isn't one single source of truth for every answer you're looking for.

344
00:34:18,880 --> 00:34:25,440
So if you want to compare creatives against each other, like MMP could be your best friend.

345
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:38,880
But if you want to compare the impact of UAC versus meta, MMP is likely not your best friend because one is a push channel, one is a pull channel, but and the competitiveness of the vertical and such.

346
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:50,240
But typically UAC is a lot cheaper because of the search and which of those higher proportion of those searches also coming from the brand search and so on.

347
00:34:51,040 --> 00:34:57,760
So you wouldn't do a budgeting decision or forecast just based on MMP and such.

348
00:34:58,080 --> 00:35:12,560
So you might want to rely on incrementality and MMM and conducting experiments around the geolift and the holdout and the blackout test and so on to come to such conclusions, knowing your baseline, knowing your seasonality.

349
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:15,520
Problems get different at different stages.

350
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:21,040
Like you don't have to worry about incrementality when you're just spending 100 K a month, I think.

351
00:35:21,680 --> 00:35:40,160
It might matter, but it matters a lot more once you get to bigger because a lot of the decisions at such a stage are driven by MMP slash last click, which is normal because when you're at that stage, you don't have all the data infrastructure set up to measure incrementality and so on.

352
00:35:40,320 --> 00:35:49,040
Like, I'm maybe generalizing a bit, but a typical startup doesn't have all those kinds of tools in house or third party like to make it happen.

353
00:35:49,680 --> 00:36:12,320
And once you get to a certain level, I would say 500 K plus, then these are becoming more, a lot more important and prominent in decisions because then you' start with the yearly budgets and then the forecasts and everything and the profitability starts mattering a lot more than before and so on, depending on, let's say, your investor vision as well.

354
00:36:13,040 --> 00:36:25,520
At the end of the day, you should just stick to one tool for certain decisions while you should also cross check with other tools time to time just as a baseline.

355
00:36:25,840 --> 00:36:29,120
I'm not saying, for example, you should use App Store Connect as your baseline.

356
00:36:29,360 --> 00:36:41,680
No, but if your MMP and Meta are very different, but your App Store Connect CPP data and meta matches that should give you also some signals.

357
00:36:42,240 --> 00:36:48,640
Take it as there are a source of truth that you should be sticking to, but don't take them blindly as this is it.

358
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:57,440
Like UAC is the best channel, let's double the budget only to see potentially the impact is output is not the double and so on.

359
00:36:57,680 --> 00:37:05,120
So that's why then it comes down to multiple tools and multiple ways of interpreting it.

360
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:22,960
For example, incrementality and last click also conflicts or MMM might be conflicting with day to day decisions as well, depending on at what level you do MMM or at what cadence you feed the MMM and how you calibrate it.

361
00:37:23,360 --> 00:37:53,360
Having worked on so many different apps and audited so many different accounts over the years, do you have any horror stories of like the data team's SQL was wrong and then for a year they were just like making really bad decisions or the MMP was not connected correctly or like, or do you have any like just crazy horror stories from all these accounts that you've worked with or multiple crazy horror stories of just making bad decisions because the process wasn't set up?

362
00:37:53,680 --> 00:37:53,760
Yeah.

363
00:37:54,160 --> 00:37:55,680
I think Star was something like this.

364
00:37:56,400 --> 00:38:01,280
They switched the MMP and then they didn't implement the events on the Neve MMP.

365
00:38:01,680 --> 00:38:08,800
I think there are probably plenty, but there are also some must avoid at all costs type of things.

366
00:38:09,600 --> 00:38:18,640
Like I remember one project switched MMPs and they contacted me after a month and said like, hi, we need some help.

367
00:38:19,120 --> 00:38:20,560
We can't figure out what's happening.

368
00:38:21,280 --> 00:38:24,000
And the issue was so obvious.

369
00:38:24,800 --> 00:38:26,160
So they switched MMPs.

370
00:38:26,800 --> 00:38:32,240
They didn't implement any events on the new MMP and all the MMP was still connected to Meta.

371
00:38:32,960 --> 00:38:35,520
They still have campaigns optimizing on Meta.

372
00:38:36,400 --> 00:38:39,440
And this is like a sizable company.

373
00:38:40,080 --> 00:38:44,400
But the problem is they have a different team handling everything MMP.

374
00:38:44,880 --> 00:38:49,600
They have a different team handling Meta only, kind of silos and stuff.

375
00:38:49,920 --> 00:38:53,840
And I was like, how did you not realize this for almost a month?

376
00:38:54,320 --> 00:38:57,600
They're like, yeah, because not our thing and so on.

377
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:01,600
So these stuff is like, I think, on a very, extreme level.

378
00:39:02,080 --> 00:39:03,200
Other than that, I don't know.

379
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:14,400
I've seen as crazy as someone spending 100 K on a creative with 100 plus dollars and didn't stop it before.

