00:58:15,040 --> 00:58:30,240
Like, Algorand is not confident to show that advertiser's ad to that user because there is much clearer signal on another advertiser's funnel based on this user's historic activity that is known to Meta.

572
00:58:31,600 --> 00:58:39,520
The Meta algorithm will rather show advertiser's B's ad to that user and not A because he doesn't know if this user will convert or not.

573
00:58:39,600 --> 00:58:44,880
And he knows that if this user will not convert on the advertiser's A's ad, he will not increase his budget.

574
00:58:45,360 --> 00:58:48,640
And Meta optimizes towards the spend.

575
00:58:49,280 --> 00:58:51,200
slash the advertiser value, right?

576
00:58:51,760 --> 00:59:07,840
So, and for Meta to work at optimal level, signal engineering is one thing, like to improve what you're sending, but fundamentally, you should not send mixed signals for the sake of what you're potentially thinking as signal engineering as well.

577
00:59:08,480 --> 00:59:14,560
So, in general, keep it simple, keep it logical, don't send all possible events because you can.

578
00:59:15,040 --> 00:59:16,240
I think that's what also people do.

579
00:59:16,400 --> 00:59:18,080
Oh, like, why don't we track also this?

580
00:59:18,400 --> 00:59:20,160
Because, look, we can send this event.

581
00:59:20,560 --> 00:59:33,120
It's difficult to quantify what I just said, but it's just talking to Facebook product managers, like knowing how this platform learns from the data, learns from the signal, and so on.

582
00:59:33,200 --> 00:59:35,760
Like this is my interpretation of that.

583
00:59:36,400 --> 00:59:39,680
And I see that the simpler accounts also do better because of that, right?

584
00:59:39,920 --> 00:59:48,000
Like it used to be different, like you had fifty types of campaigns and so on, now like you have the broad interest and the lookalike that is all you need.

585
00:59:48,560 --> 00:59:59,600
Of course there are more, but I'm just simplifying past versus now because algos know exactly who to go after based on the signals that it gets and based on the signals that it has on the user.

586
01:00:00,400 --> 01:00:10,720
So the signals that your app has, if it's totally different than what users engage with all the other apps and all the other businesses, then there's a mismatch, right?

587
01:00:10,960 --> 01:00:12,640
And that's a whole business model of Meta.

588
01:00:13,040 --> 01:00:22,720
It will find exactly the user it learns from the users that it showed impression to, it gets the conversion as a feedback, and then it will try to find more similar people.

589
01:00:23,440 --> 01:00:33,520
So when you send us mixed signals, mixed funnel that doesn't make sense from the auction calculation point of view, then you're also potentially losing out on this kind of opportunities.

590
01:00:33,840 --> 01:00:40,000
So having said that, like if you're happy with your your current event mapping, don't try to just challenge it for the sake of challenging.

591
01:00:40,640 --> 01:00:50,960
If you're unhappy and thinking there is a room to try something different, I would suggest create another event that gets triggered at the same place.

592
01:00:51,280 --> 01:01:04,720
For example, the trial start, add another trial start event in your MMP, then map that to, let's say, to subscribe, then run a split test purchase versus subscribe to see, like, which event is more resonating.

593
01:01:04,960 --> 01:01:17,760
Of course, like the history of each will not be the same, so I would at least wait a week or or two so that the event manager will build some history and understand what does this event mean for their algorithm and so on.

594
01:01:18,080 --> 01:01:25,920
Again, it's difficult to quantify wait one week versus two weeks, but it's just to build some data history and then run the test and then see how it is.

595
01:01:26,080 --> 01:01:32,000
And that's exactly what we did for this particular project and then we saw a 35% improvement just like that.

596
01:01:32,240 --> 01:01:44,720
